{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its massing and siting, would present an&nbsp;inappropriate development&nbsp;that would disrupt the established pattern of dwellings with direct street frontages and organised building lines. Its jagged building line would conflict with the existing built form of the area and would fail to integrate with the prevailing built form. It would have little street presence and would&nbsp;create a visual block due to its forward siting against the neighbouring flats.&nbsp;As such, the proposal would appear incongruous and discordant, causing harm to the visual amenity, openness, and spatial character of the area, contrary to:Paragraphs 131 and 135 of the National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December 2024)Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP2, DMD1 and DMD4 of the LBBD Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposed external amenity space would be unacceptable and would fail to present a safe and secure outdoor space for future residents.&nbsp; The proximity to the highway and pavement is likely to be impacted by noise and would not provide an adequate outdoor space. The&nbsp;amenity space would be overlooked by the front facing windows of the flat block. As such, the proposal would present a poor quality of external amenity, contrary to:National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December 2024)Policy D6 of the London Plan (March 2021)",
    "The proposed development fails to provide clarity on the proposed parking and access arrangements and fails to demonstrate whether the area could facilitate the demand for off-street parking at this site or whether it could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. Officers are unable to determine whether the proposal would result in parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies:National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December 2024)Policies T1 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)",
    "The proposed development would be&nbsp;at odds with the surrounding pattern of development and would&nbsp;in harm to neighbouring occupiers particularly nos.123-129 Goresbrook Road, resulting in a loss of outlook and causing an overbearing impact.&nbsp;The additional generation of noise, comings and goings and general disturbances will also result in harm, noting the atypical access arrangement.&nbsp;The development would therefore constitute&nbsp;unneighbourly&nbsp;development, and would be contrary to:National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (December 2024)Policies D6 and D14 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies DMD 1 and DMSI 3 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
